From: Azure Dreams <azuredreams@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 23:13:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] HD-2D from the scene's depth, and a "time" uniform for
 the shaders

The HD-2D chain now works from the scene's depth, which the core hands over in
the frame's alpha (SwanStation's scene depth: the square root of PGXP's depth
for each pixel of the 3D scene, 0 where something flat was drawn last), instead
of guessing at the picture:

- The focus is the scene's depth around where the player stands. Each pixel of
  the scene is blurred by how far its own depth is from it, nearer or farther,
  so the depth of field follows every model's edge.
- The blur only gathers out-of-focus scene samples: what's sharp, characters
  and the interface among it, never smears into it.
- Anything flat (the interface, 2D sprites) is shown exactly as the game drew
  it: no blur, glow, grading, vignette or specks of light.
- Glow comes from the scene's bright parts only; drifting specks of light are
  drawn in the scene, under the interface.

Shaders get a "time" uniform (seconds of frames drawn, at 60 a second) for the
drifting light.
